amux licensing
==============

This repository holds two kinds of software under two different licences.
Which one applies depends only on where a file lives.

Everything outside `apps/`
--------------------------

The amux core — the multiplexer, the daemon, the protocols, the transport,
the terminal client, the libraries and the tooling — is dual-licensed under
either of

  * MIT license ([LICENSE-MIT](LICENSE-MIT))
  * Apache License, Version 2.0 ([LICENSE-APACHE](LICENSE-APACHE))

at your option. This is open source, and it is the same choice the Rust
ecosystem offers, so amux composes with it without anyone having to think
about licences.

Unless you state otherwise, any contribution you intentionally submit for
inclusion in this part of the work, as defined in the Apache-2.0 licence,
is dual licensed as above, with no additional terms or conditions.

Everything inside `apps/`
-------------------------

The applications — currently the iPhone app, at `apps/apple/` — are licensed
under the Functional Source License, version 1.1, with an Apache 2.0 future licence
(FSL-1.1-ALv2). See [apps/LICENSE](apps/LICENSE) for the terms.

The FSL is **source-available, not open source**. You may read, build,
modify and redistribute the application code for any purpose except
competing with us: broadly, shipping it to other people as a product or
service that substitutes for amux or for what we offer using it. Internal
use, non-commercial research and education, and professional services
around amux are all explicitly permitted.

Every version becomes Apache-2.0 two years after we publish it. That clock
runs per version, from the day the code is made available — for this
repository, the day a commit is pushed. Nothing has to be tracked or
renewed, and no date has to be nominated: a commit two years old is
Apache-2.0, whatever has happened to the app since.

Why the split
-------------

amux is meant to be a utility other software can depend on, so its core
asks nothing of you. The applications are the product, and the FSL protects
them from being resold out from under us while still letting you read the
source, build it yourself, fix it, and keep using what you have built. The
two-year conversion is the promise that this protection expires rather than
accumulates.

[docs/LICENSING.md](docs/LICENSING.md) answers the questions this summary
raises.
